WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:01.030 --> 00:00:04.019
Leah: Most young people experience
ups and downs during their childhood

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:07.400
and teenage years and occasionally
will feel down or upset by certain

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:08.799
things going on in their lives.

00:00:09.530 --> 00:00:13.600
Rhiannon: Feeling sad or fed up is
a normal reaction to experiences

00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:15.140
that are difficult or stressful.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:19.430
A low mood often involves
sadness, an anxious feeling,

00:00:19.430 --> 00:00:20.790
worry and low self-esteem.

00:00:20.790 --> 00:00:23.169
It may even involve frustration and anger.

00:00:23.169 --> 00:00:27.249
Leah: However, it will tend to improve
after a short time, especially if

00:00:27.249 --> 00:00:31.309
the child or young person makes
changes to resolve their problems,

00:00:31.759 --> 00:00:35.419
by discussing them, facing them
head on or even getting more sleep.

00:00:36.500 --> 00:00:39.760
If children and young people are able
to challenge their negative thoughts

00:00:39.820 --> 00:00:43.850
and replace them with positivity, they
are more likely to improve their mood.

00:00:44.790 --> 00:00:49.110
Rhiannon: But some young people feel sad,
lonely, down, and anxious or stressed

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:52.909
for much longer periods of time, to
the extent that it can affect their

00:00:52.949 --> 00:00:56.730
everyday lives and can prevent them from
doing things they would normally do.

00:00:57.140 --> 00:01:00.449
Leah: It is then, when a low mood
doesn’t go away and if anything, gets

00:01:00.450 --> 00:01:04.540
worse, that it needs to be looked at
as clinical depression and a mental

00:01:04.540 --> 00:01:06.220
condition that needs to be addressed.

